 Conversely, let $\tilde F(z)=\frac{\tilde f(l_u(z))}{l_u(z)}z\in\mathcal{S}^*(\mathbb{B})$ and $F(z)=\frac{ f(l_u(z))}{l_u(z)}z$, where $f$ is given by \eqref{Th-2.0}. According to Lemma 2.1 \cite{Xu-He-Xu}, we have $\tilde f\in\mathcal{S}^*(\mathbb{U})$. Consequently \eqref{Th-2.2} holds, where $p\in\mathcal{P}$. Using \eqref{Th-2.0}, one can easily arrive at \eqref{Th-2.4}. Then by a simple computation, we can easily arrive at \eqref{Th-2.1}. Finally, we conclude that $F(z)=\frac{f(l_{u}(z))}{l_{u}(z)}z\in\mathcal{M}_{\alpha}(\mathbb{B})$.

\label{Th-31}Let $f\in\mathcal{H}(\mathbb{B},\mathbb{C})$ with $f(0)=1$ and $F(z)=zf(z)\in\mathcal{M}_{\alpha}(\mathbb{B})$, $\alpha\geq 0$. Then for $z\in\mathbb{B}\backslash\{0\}$, we have
